{parent=Philosophy}
{wiki}

= Sceptical scenario
{parent=Epistemology}

= Aristotelian logic
{parent=Philosophy}
{c}

= Fallibilism
{parent=Philosophy}
{wiki}

A position allowing warranted belief while recognizing that its justification can be mistaken.

= Philosophical skepticism
{parent=Philosophy}
{wiki}

= Philosophical scepticism
{synonym}

Challenges to the possibility or justification of knowledge. Global challenges should be distinguished from doubts about a particular instrument or inference.

= Empiricism
{parent=Philosophy}
{wiki}

= Empiricist
{parent=Empiricism}

= Observable phenomenon
{parent=Empiricism}

= Observability
{parent=Observable phenomenon}
{disambiguate=philosophy of science}

= Observability
{synonym}

= Philosophy of science
{parent=Philosophy}

= Abductive reasoning
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Scientific research programme
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Idols of the mind
{parent=Philosophy of science}

<Francis Bacon>'s classification of recurrent intellectual distortions associated with human tendencies, individual dispositions, language and received systems. It forms part of his programme for correcting inquiry.<Novum Organum by Francis Bacon>[Book I, aphorisms 38–44]{ref}

= Instrumentalism
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Context of justification
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Context of discovery
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Scientific scepticism
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Hypothesis
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Scientific law
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Logical positivism
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Logical empiricism
{synonym}

= Analytic-synthetic distinction
{parent=Logical positivism}

= Meaning postulate
{parent=Analytic-synthetic distinction}
{wiki}

A rule constraining the admissible <interpretations> of predicates in a stated formal language. A sentence can then count as analytic relative to the chosen rules if it holds in every admitted interpretation. This precise relative notion does not itself establish an unrevisable division of all natural-language statements.<Meaning Postulates by Rudolf Carnap>[sections 1–3]{ref}

= Synthetic truth
{parent=Analytic-synthetic distinction}

= Analytic truth
{parent=Analytic-synthetic distinction}

= Scientific progress
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Scientific underdetermination
{parent=Philosophy of science}

An evidential situation in which rival scientific accounts remain compatible with the relevant evidence. Agreement with the observations so far is weaker than <empirical equivalence> across all possible observations in a stated domain.

= Revolutionary theory change
{parent=Philosophy of science}

A substantial reorganization of scientific concepts, accepted problems or methods. This generic notion is distinct from the particular early modern historical episode called the <Scientific Revolution>.

= Incommensurability
{disambiguate=philosophy of science}
{parent=Revolutionary theory change}

= Incommensurability
{synonym}

= Normal science
{parent=Revolutionary theory change}
{wiki}

In <Thomas Kuhn>'s account, research conducted within an accepted framework of exemplary achievements, problems and methods.<The Structure of Scientific Revolutions by Thomas Kuhn>[Introduction]{ref}

= Paradigm
{disambiguate=science}
{parent=Normal science}

= Scientific paradigm
{synonym}

= Cause
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Causal power
{parent=Cause}

= Necessary connection
{parent=Cause}

= Experiment
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

A deliberately arranged intervention or comparison used to investigate a claim. A useful <experiment> specifies the conditions changed, the outcome observed and the alternative explanations the comparison can discriminate.

= Structuralism
{disambiguate=philosophy of science}
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Structural realism
{parent=Structuralism (philosophy of science)}

A form of <scientific realism> emphasizing warranted knowledge of physical relations or structure despite changes in proposed underlying entities. An account must specify which interpreted relations survive and in which domains, rather than merely point to recurring notation.<Structural Realism: The Best of Both Worlds? by John Worrall>[discussion of the Fresnel–Maxwell transition]{ref}

= Empirical equivalence
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Empirically equivalent
{synonym}

Two scientific accounts are empirically equivalent relative to a stated domain when they give the same observable consequences there. Agreement on the observations so far is weaker than agreement on every possible observation.

= Scientific confirmation
{parent=Philosophy of science}

Evidence supports a scientific hypothesis without necessarily proving it. Distinguish increasing its credibility from showing it highly credible in absolute terms; a low <prior probability> can rise substantially while remaining low.

= Inductive reasoning
{parent=Scientific confirmation}
{wiki}

Inference beyond what the premises deductively entail, often from observed cases to unobserved cases. This is distinct from <mathematical induction>.

= Problem of induction
{parent=Inductive reasoning}
{wiki}

The challenge of justifying <inductive reasoning> without either assuming the reliability being justified or demanding a <deduction> from premises that do not entail the conclusion.<An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding by David Hume>[section IV, part II]{ref}

= Duhem problem
{c}
{parent=Scientific confirmation}

From $T\land A\Rightarrow E$ and $\neg E$, infer only $\neg T\lor\neg A$. A failed prediction therefore does not logically isolate failure of the target $T$ from failure of <auxiliary hypotheses> $A$. Independent calibration and varied experiments can discriminate packages; the logical ambiguity does not imply that every package is equally supported.<The Aim and Structure of Physical Theory by Pierre Duhem>[part II, chapter VI, section 2]{ref}

= Auxiliary hypothesis
{parent=Scientific confirmation}

An assumption used with a target scientific hypothesis to obtain a prediction, for example an apparatus calibration or an initial condition. Independent evidence for an <auxiliary hypothesis> helps distinguish a failure of the target from a failure of the test setup.

= Ad hoc hypothesis
{parent=Auxiliary hypothesis}
{wiki}

= Bayesian confirmation
{c}
{parent=Scientific confirmation}
{title2=$P(H\mid E,K)>P(H\mid K)$}

Evidence $E$ confirms $H$ relative to background $K$ when it increases its <conditional probability>. With $0<P(H\mid K)<1$ and positive evidence <probability>, <Bayes' theorem> gives
$$
P(H\mid E,K)>P(H\mid K)\iff P(E\mid H,K)>P(E\mid\neg H,K).
$$
Indeed, writing $p=P(H\mid K)$, $a=P(E\mid H,K)$ and $b=P(E\mid\neg H,K)$ gives the <posterior probability> $pa/(pa+(1-p)b)$; comparison with $p$ reduces to $a>b$. Thus <scientific confirmation> depends on the alternatives and background, not just on a statement's logical form.

= Finite-population model of raven confirmation
{parent=Bayesian confirmation}
{title2=$L_{\mathrm{nonblack}}=(1-1/M)^{-1},\quad L_{\mathrm{raven}}=(1-1/r)^{-1}$}

Fix $r>1$ <ravens> and $M>1$ nonblack objects in a finite population. Under $H_0$, all <ravens> are black; under $H_1$, exactly one is nonblack. The total numbers $r,M$ are held fixed under both <hypotheses>, so the classification of one nonraven also changes between hypotheses. This is a deliberately specified model for <Bayesian confirmation>, not a universal rule about how bird observations supply evidence.

A uniform draw among nonblack objects returns a nonraven with <probabilities> $1$ under $H_0$ and $1-1/M$ under $H_1$. A uniform draw among <ravens> returns a black object with <probabilities> $1$ and $1-1/r$. The respective <likelihood ratios> for $H_0$ over $H_1$ are therefore $(1-1/M)^{-1}$ and $(1-1/r)^{-1}$. When $M>r$, the former is closer to one, so supplies less support under these assumptions.

With equal <prior probabilities>, <Bayes' theorem> gives <posterior probabilities> $1/(2-1/M)$ and $1/(2-1/r)$. More generally, with <prior probability> $p$ for $H_0$, replace the two expressions by $p/[p+(1-p)(1-1/M)]$ and $p/[p+(1-p)(1-1/r)]$. Previously known information or a different sampling protocol requires different <likelihoods>; selecting an object already known to be a nonraven need not supply new evidence.

= Problem of old evidence
{parent=Bayesian confirmation}

Already accepted evidence can support a newly considered theory even though conditioning again on a fact of <probability> one produces no increase in its <posterior probability>. Distinguish new learning, retrospective support and discovering a previously unrecognized theory–evidence connection.<Why I Am Not a Bayesian by Clark Glymour>[chapter 3 of Theory and Evidence]{ref}

= Raven paradox
{parent=Scientific confirmation}
{wiki}

“All ravens are black” is <logically equivalent> to “all nonblack things are nonravens”. An unrestricted rule that positive instances confirm universal generalizations would make a white shoe confirm the first statement. A <Bayesian confirmation> account instead conditions on background knowledge and the sampling procedure.<Studies in the Logic of Confirmation I by Carl Hempel>[section 5]{ref}

= Scientific explanation
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Explanatory asymmetry
{parent=Scientific explanation}

Some physical relations permit <deduction> in both directions while supporting different explanatory roles. Pole height and solar angle can explain a shadow length even though the height can also be inferred from that length. An evidential inference need not have the same direction as the <causal explanation>.

= Explanatory unification
{parent=Scientific explanation}

= Causal explanation
{parent=Scientific explanation}

= Causal relevance
{parent=Causal explanation}

= Covering-law model
{parent=Scientific explanation}

An approach to <scientific explanation> that subsumes a phenomenon under general laws and specified circumstances. Its deductive form is the <deductive-nomological model>; statistical forms permit probabilistic support rather than entailment.

= Inductive-statistical model
{parent=Covering-law model}

A form of <scientific explanation> using statistical laws and high <conditional probability>, relative to appropriate available information. A low-probability event can nevertheless have a <mechanistic explanation>, so high <probability> is not an uncontested necessary condition for explanation.

= Deductive-nomological model
{parent=Covering-law model}
{wiki}

An explanation derives an event description from laws and circumstances, with valid <deduction>, essential use of a law, empirical content and true premises. These requirements do not by themselves settle explanatory direction or eliminate irrelevant additions.<Studies in the Logic of Explanation by Carl Hempel and Paul Oppenheim>[section 3]{ref}

= Demarcation problem
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Verificationism
{parent=Demarcation problem}
{wiki}

Distinguish a criterion of empirical meaningfulness from a methodological demand for verification, and conclusive verification from fallible <scientific confirmation>.

= Falsifiability
{parent=Demarcation problem}
{wiki}

A hypothesis is falsifiable if some possible empirical outcomes conflict with it under specified testing assumptions. <Falsifiability> does not mean that an anomalous reading identifies which member of a theoretical package is false, or that a falsifiable statement is well supported.

= Falsificationism
{parent=Falsifiability}

A methodology emphasizing conjectures exposed to demanding attempts at refutation. <Falsifiability> is a property of claims under testing assumptions; falsificationism is a view about scientific practice.

= Pseudoscience
{parent=Demarcation problem}
{wiki}

= Theory-ladenness
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

The dependence of scientific observation, description or interpretation on background concepts and assumptions. Dependence on some theory is weaker than dependence on the truth of the particular theory being tested.

= Constructive empiricism
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

= Constructive empiricist
{synonym}

Acceptance of a scientific theory involves belief in its <empirical adequacy> and practical commitment to its use, without requiring belief in its full truth about unobservables. This concerns epistemic attitudes, whereas the distinction between a <constructive theory> and a <principle theory> concerns explanatory organization.<Constructive Empiricism Now by Bas van Fraassen>[section III]{ref}

= Empirical adequacy
{parent=Constructive empiricism}

= Empirically adequate
{synonym}

A theory is empirically adequate when it correctly represents the observable phenomena, including those not yet observed. Agreement with the data already collected is therefore insufficient to establish <empirical adequacy>.

= Natural kind
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

A classification whose usefulness in prediction and explanation is grounded in relevant natural structure, rather than solely in a convenient label. Exact membership conditions can help identify a <natural kind> but do not alone demonstrate its explanatory significance.

= Homeostatic property cluster theory
{parent=Natural kind}
{wiki}

An account of some <natural kinds> in terms of traits whose association is sustained by causal processes, without requiring every member to possess every trait. For <species>, shared descent, <gene flow> and developmental processes can sustain relevant similarities. Whether a cluster supports reliable inference is an empirical issue.<Homeostasis, Species, and Higher Taxa by Richard Boyd>[sections 0.0–0.1]{ref}

= Scientific realism
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki}

A family of positions taking scientific theories to make literal claims about a mind-independent world, with warranted belief extending to at least some unobservable aspects. The extent of that commitment and the meaning of approximate truth require specification.

= Scientific realist
{parent=Scientific realism}

= Approximate truth
{parent=Scientific realism}

= Unobservable entity
{parent=Scientific realism}

= No-miracles argument
{parent=Scientific realism}

= Pessimistic induction
{parent=Scientific realism}

= Entity realism
{parent=Scientific realism}
{wiki}

= Primary-secondary quality distinction
{parent=Philosophy of science}
{wiki=Primary–secondary_quality_distinction}

= Primary and secondary qualities
{synonym}

In an account of perception, distinguish an object's physical constitution from its powers to produce experiences in perceivers. The distinction between <primary qualities> and <secondary qualities> does not by itself prove an external world or imply that dispositions are unreal.<An Essay Concerning Human Understanding by John Locke>[book II, chapter VIII, sections 8–15 and 23–26]{ref}

= Secondary quality
{parent=Primary-secondary quality distinction}

In <John Locke>'s account, a body's power to produce an experience such as a colour or taste through its physical constitution. Distinguish that power from the experience itself.

= Primary quality
{parent=Primary-secondary quality distinction}

In <John Locke>'s account, a quality such as extension, solidity, shape, number or <motion>, attributed to bodies independently of whether they are perceived. This historical list should not be treated as an unrevisable inventory of fundamental physical properties.

= Scientific reproducibility
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Scientific replication
{parent=Scientific reproducibility}

Testing a scientific finding with newly collected data or an independently conducted study. Terminology varies, so the operational meaning should be stated.

= Computational reproducibility
{parent=Scientific reproducibility}

Obtaining consistent computational results from the same input data, code and stated computational conditions. Exact bitwise agreement is stronger than agreement to a justified numerical or statistical tolerance.

= Data provenance
{parent=Computational reproducibility}

The recorded origins and transformations of data, including references, versions, preprocessing and identifiers.

= Mechanistic explanation
{parent=Philosophy of science}

An explanation in terms of entities, activities and their organization that produce a phenomenon.

= Reductionism
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Explanatory reductionism
{parent=Reductionism}

The stronger claim that higher-level explanations can or should be replaced by explanations in terms of more basic components and their interactions.

= Ontological reductionism
{parent=Reductionism}

A claim about what entities and processes are made of, distinguished from a claim that every useful explanation must be expressed at the microscopic level.

= Philosophy of biology
{parent=Philosophy of science}

= Biological function
{parent=Philosophy of biology}

A role of a biological feature, interpreted either through its contribution to a current system or through its evolutionary history. A claim about function does not imply foresight in evolution.

= Multiple realizability
{parent=Philosophy of biology}

A higher-level relationship or function can be implemented by different underlying physical mechanisms.

= Explanatory autonomy
{parent=Philosophy of biology}

The capacity of a field to frame and test useful explanations at its own level of description, consistently with the physical realization of its objects.

= Physicalism
{parent=Philosophy of biology}

The view that living systems are physically realized and do not require a separate nonphysical substance. This does not by itself settle which level of description provides the best scientific explanation.

= Philosophy bibliography
{parent=Philosophy}

= Commensurability, Comparability, Communicability by Thomas Kuhn
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Thomas Kuhn}
{title2=1982 proceedings; published 1983}

https://www.marcellodibello.com/commonground/readings/KuhnComComCom.pdf
https://www.jstor.org/stable/192452

Primary paper, PSA 1982, volume 2, pp. 669–688; the scanned publication carries 1983 copyright. The discussion distinguishes local translation difficulties from interpretation and learning a conceptual vocabulary. The chemical example connects <phlogiston>, element and principle.

= Experimentation and Scientific Realism by Ian Hacking
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Ian Hacking}
{title2=1982}

https://web.stanford.edu/~paulsko/papers/Hacking.pdf

Original article, Philosophical Topics 13(1), 1982, pp. 71–87. The opening distinguishes experimental entities used as tools from belief in the truth of whole theories. A related chapter appears in the 1983 book Representing and Intervening; its pagination is different.

= What Is Mathematical Truth? by Hilary Putnam
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Hilary Putnam}
{title2=1975; linked second edition 1979}

https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511625268.006

Chapter 4 of Mathematics, Matter and Method, pp. 60–78; printed p. 73 gives the realist argument from scientific success. The chapter appeared in the 1975 collection; the linked publisher record is for the 1979 second edition and its later online publication. The similarly named short Historia Mathematica item is not silently substituted.

