{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Rudolf Carnap}
{title2=1952}
{title2=Philosophical Studies 3:65–73}

Original article scan: https://liarparadox.org/Meaning_Postulates_Rudolf_Carnap_1952.pdf

= Two Dogmas of Empiricism by Willard Van Orman Quine
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Willard Van Orman Quine}
{title2=1951}

Original essay text, reproduced in its revised collected form: https://faculty.fiu.edu/~harrisk/Notes/Epistemology/1141%20PHI3300%20Readings%2015%20Quine%20Two%20Dogmas%20of%20Empiricism.htm[Institutionally hosted text]. Sections I–IV challenge proposed explanations of analyticity; sections V–VI address reductionism and the wider system tested by experience.

= The Scientific Image by Bas van Fraassen
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Bas van Fraassen}
{title2=1980}
{title2=10.1093/0198244274.001.0001}

https://academic.oup.com/book/7116[Publisher record and synopsis]. https://www.princeton.edu/~fraassen/Sci-Img/[Author's book page]. Chapter 2 introduces <constructive empiricism>; the publisher's synopsis explicitly distinguishes literal theoretical language, <empirical adequacy> and theory acceptance.

= Why I Am Not a Bayesian by Clark Glymour
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by Clark Glymour}
{title2=1980}

Chapter 3 of *Theory and Evidence*. https://www.andrew.cmu.edu/user/kk3n/simplicity/glymour1980c3.pdf[Institutionally hosted scan of the chapter]. The old-evidence/new-theory discussion concerns the inability of ordinary conditioning on already certain evidence to represent its newly recognized support for a theory.

= An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding by David Hume
{parent=Philosophy bibliography}
{c}
{tag=Work by David Hume}
{title2=1748}

https://davidhume.org/texts/e/4[Original text of section IV: Sceptical Doubts concerning the Operations of the Understanding]. Parts I–II distinguish demonstrative reasoning from inference beyond experience and examine the circular appeal to past success.

